  "A2-1": {"id": "A2-1", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A2", "step": 1, "stepName": "Carbon environments", "question": "How many carbon environments does this ¹³C spectrum show?", "options": ["7", "8", "9", "11"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Count the lines: 200.9, 142.3, 134.7, 130.4, 129.0, 128.3, 126.2, 34.3 and 8.3 ppm make nine. The compound has 11 carbons, but a monosubstituted benzene ring has a mirror plane, so its two ortho carbons are equivalent and so are its two meta carbons, leaving four ring environments rather than six. The crowded lines between 126 and 135 ppm are the easiest to miscount, so check the labels.", "image": "spectra/A2-1.png", "caption": "¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum, 101 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum (101 MHz, CDCl₃) with lines at 200.9, 142.3, 134.7, 130.4, 129.0, 128.3, 126.2, 34.3 and 8.3 ppm."},
  "A2-2": {"id": "A2-2", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A2", "step": 2, "stepName": "Integration", "question": "The integrals read 1.01 : 0.98 : 0.33 : 0.67 : 1.00. Which ratio of hydrogens fits C₁₁H₁₂O?", "options": ["3 : 3 : 1 : 2 : 3", "2 : 2 : 1 : 1 : 2", "3 : 3 : 2 : 1 : 3", "6 : 6 : 2 : 4 : 6"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "The smallest integral, 0.33, must be at least one hydrogen, so multiply everything by three: 1.01 : 0.98 : 0.33 : 0.67 : 1.00 becomes about 3 : 3 : 1 : 2 : 3, a total of 12, matching C₁₁H₁₂O. Doubling and rounding gives 2 : 2 : 1 : 1 : 2, only 8 hydrogens. The first integral is 3H because one alkene hydrogen sits inside the ring multiplet.", "image": "spectra/A2-2.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₁₁H₁₂O, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 7.60–7.52 (several overlapping signals), 7.43–7.35 (several overlapping signals), 6.75, 2.70 and 1.17 ppm; integrals under them read 1.01, 0.98, 0.33, 0.67, 1.00 from left to right."},
  "A2-3": {"id": "A2-3", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A2", "step": 3, "stepName": "Chemical shift", "question": "What does the shift of the 2H signal at 2.70 ppm tell you?", "options": ["The CH₂ is bonded to an oxygen", "The CH₂ is part of a C=C double bond", "The CH₂ is in an alkyl chain, away from any O or C=O", "The CH₂ is next to the C=O group"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "A CH₂ next to a carbonyl group is deshielded to about 2.0 to 2.7 ppm, so 2.70 ppm fits CH₂–C=O. A CH₂ bonded to oxygen would appear near 3.5 to 4.5 ppm, alkene hydrogens above 4.5 ppm, and an alkyl CH₂ well away from O or C=O near 1.3 ppm. With the CH₃ triplet at 1.17 ppm, this is a CH₂CH₃ group attached to the C=O.", "image": "spectra/A2-3.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₁₁H₁₂O, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 7.60–7.52 (several overlapping signals), 7.43–7.35 (several overlapping signals), 6.75, 2.70 and 1.17 ppm; integrals under them read 1.01, 0.98, 0.33, 0.67, 1.00 from left to right. The signal at 2.70 ppm is marked."},
  "A2-4": {"id": "A2-4", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A2", "step": 4, "stepName": "Coupling", "question": "The enlarged signal has lines at 2708.1 and 2691.9 Hz. What is J?", "options": ["8.1 Hz", "16.2 Hz", "32.4 Hz", "2700.0 Hz"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "J is the gap between the two lines of the doublet in hertz: 2708.1 − 2691.9 = 16.2 Hz. Use the full gap, not the distance from one line to the centre, and 2700.0 Hz is where the signal sits, not how it is split. Coupling constants are quoted in hertz because, unlike chemical shifts in ppm, they do not change with the field strength of the spectrometer.", "image": "spectra/A2-4.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₁₁H₁₂O, 400 MHz, CDCl₃, with the signal at 6.75 ppm enlarged. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 7.60–7.52 (several overlapping signals), 7.43–7.35 (several overlapping signals), 6.75, 2.70 and 1.17 ppm; integrals under them read 1.01, 0.98, 0.33, 0.67, 1.00 from left to right. An enlarged view shows the signal at 6.75 ppm as two lines of equal height at 2708.1 and 2691.9 Hz."},
  "A2-5": {"id": "A2-5", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A2", "step": 5, "stepName": "Structure", "question": "Which compound gives these spectra?", "options": [["(Z)-1-Phenylpent-1-en-3-one", "PhCH=CHCOCH₂CH₃"], ["(E)-1-Phenylpent-1-en-3-one", "PhCH=CHCOCH₂CH₃"], ["(E)-1-Phenylpent-2-en-1-one", "PhCOCH=CHCH₂CH₃"], ["(E)-4-Phenylpent-3-en-2-one", "PhC(CH₃)=CHCOCH₃"]], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The alkene hydrogens couple with J = 16.2 Hz. Trans couplings across a C=C are typically 12 to 18 Hz and cis couplings 6 to 12 Hz, so this C=C is E. The CH₂ quartet at 2.70 ppm sits next to the C=O, which rules out 1-phenylpent-2-en-1-one, and 4-phenylpent-3-en-2-one would show only one alkene hydrogen and two CH₃ singlets.", "image": "spectra/A2-5.png", "caption": "¹H (400 MHz, top) and ¹³C{¹H} (101 MHz, bottom) NMR spectra of C₁₁H₁₂O, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, top) with signals at 7.60–7.52 (several overlapping signals), 7.43–7.35 (several overlapping signals), 6.75, 2.70 and 1.17 ppm; integrals under them read 1.01, 0.98, 0.33, 0.67, 1.00 from left to right; and ¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum (101 MHz, bottom) with lines at 200.9, 142.3, 134.7, 130.4, 129.0, 128.3, 126.2, 34.3 and 8.3 ppm; both in CDCl₃."},
