  "A3-2": {"id": "A3-2", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A3", "step": 2, "stepName": "Integration", "question": "The integrals read 0.50 : 1.00 : 0.99 : 1.52. Which ratio of hydrogens fits C₈H₈O₂?", "options": ["0.5 : 1 : 1 : 1.5", "1 : 2 : 2 : 3", "1 : 2 : 3 : 2", "2 : 4 : 4 : 6"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Scale the integrals until they are whole numbers that add up to the formula. Doubling 0.50 : 1.00 : 0.99 : 1.52 gives about 1 : 2 : 2 : 3, a total of 8, matching the eight hydrogens in C₈H₈O₂. 0.5 : 1 : 1 : 1.5 keeps the proportions but totals only 4, 2 : 4 : 4 : 6 totals 16, and 1 : 2 : 3 : 2 does not follow the order of the integrals.", "image": "spectra/A3-2.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₈H₈O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 9.88, 7.84, 7.00 and 3.89 ppm; integrals under them read 0.50, 1.00, 0.99, 1.52 from left to right."},
  "A3-3": {"id": "A3-3", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A3", "step": 3, "stepName": "Chemical shift", "question": "What does the shift of the 1H signal at 9.88 ppm tell you?", "options": ["It is an aldehyde hydrogen, H–C=O", "It is a carboxylic acid O–H", "It is a ring hydrogen next to the C=O", "It is an alkene hydrogen"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Aldehyde hydrogens are strongly deshielded by the C=O group and appear at about 9.5 to 10.1 ppm, so a sharp 1H singlet at 9.88 ppm points to H–C=O. A carboxylic acid O–H is usually a broad signal above 10.5 ppm, ring hydrogens stay below about 8.2 ppm even next to a C=O, and alkene hydrogens appear at 4.5 to 7.5 ppm.", "image": "spectra/A3-3.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₈H₈O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 9.88, 7.84, 7.00 and 3.89 ppm; integrals under them read 0.50, 1.00, 0.99, 1.52 from left to right. The signal at 9.88 ppm is marked."},
  "A3-4": {"id": "A3-4", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A3", "step": 4, "stepName": "Coupling", "question": "What does the splitting of this signal show?", "options": ["It is coupled to the aldehyde hydrogen", "It has no hydrogens on the neighbouring carbon", "It has 1 hydrogen on the neighbouring carbon", "It has 2 hydrogens on the neighbouring carbon"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "The enlarged signal is a doublet: two lines of equal height. By the n + 1 rule, two lines means one neighbouring hydrogen, so each of these ring hydrogens has one hydrogen beside it. In a para-disubstituted ring the four ring hydrogens form two pairs, and each pair appears as a doublet. Coupling to the aldehyde hydrogen is too small to split the signal visibly.", "image": "spectra/A3-4.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₈H₈O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃, with the signal at 7.84 ppm enlarged. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 9.88, 7.84, 7.00 and 3.89 ppm; integrals under them read 0.50, 1.00, 0.99, 1.52 from left to right. An enlarged view shows the signal at 7.84 ppm as two lines of equal height."},
  "A3-5": {"id": "A3-5", "set": "A", "spectrum": "A3", "step": 5, "stepName": "Structure", "question": "Which compound gives these spectra?", "options": [["2-Methoxybenzaldehyde", "2-CH₃OC₆H₄CHO"], ["Methyl benzoate", "C₆H₅CO₂CH₃"], ["4-Methylbenzoic acid", "4-CH₃C₆H₄CO₂H"], ["4-Methoxybenzaldehyde", "4-CH₃OC₆H₄CHO"]], "correct": 3, "explanation": "The 1H singlet at 9.88 ppm is an aldehyde, the 3H singlet at 3.89 ppm an OCH₃, and two 2H doublets show a para-disubstituted ring, so this is 4-methoxybenzaldehyde. The 2-isomer would give four separate ring signals, methyl benzoate a 5H ring pattern and no aldehyde, and 4-methylbenzoic acid a CH₃ near 2.4 ppm and a broad O–H above 10.5 ppm.", "image": "spectra/A3-5.png", "caption": "¹H (400 MHz, top) and ¹³C{¹H} (101 MHz, bottom) NMR spectra of C₈H₈O₂, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, top) with signals at 9.88, 7.84, 7.00 and 3.89 ppm; integrals under them read 0.50, 1.00, 0.99, 1.52 from left to right; and ¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum (101 MHz, bottom) with lines at 190.9, 164.6, 132.0, 130.0, 114.3 and 55.6 ppm; both in CDCl₃."},
  "B1-1": {"id": "B1-1", "set": "B", "spectrum": "B1", "step": 1, "stepName": "Carbon environments", "question": "How many carbon environments does this ¹³C spectrum show?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "10"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Count one carbon environment per line: 174.2, 51.5, 36.0, 18.5 and 13.7 ppm make five. The weak line at 174.2 ppm is the carbonyl carbon, which carries no hydrogens and so gives a small signal that is easy to overlook. Five lines for a five-carbon compound means every carbon is in a different environment; there is no symmetry to make two carbons equivalent.", "image": "spectra/B1-1.png", "caption": "¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum, 101 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹³C{¹H} NMR spectrum (101 MHz, CDCl₃) with lines at 174.2, 51.5, 36.0, 18.5 and 13.7 ppm."},
  "B1-2": {"id": "B1-2", "set": "B", "spectrum": "B1", "step": 2, "stepName": "Integration", "question": "The integrals read 1.48 : 1.00 : 0.99 : 1.51. Which ratio of hydrogens fits C₅H₁₀O₂?", "options": ["3 : 2 : 2 : 3", "1.5 : 1 : 1 : 1.5", "2 : 3 : 3 : 2", "6 : 4 : 4 : 6"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Scale the integrals until they are whole numbers that add up to the formula. Doubling 1.48 : 1.00 : 0.99 : 1.51 gives about 3 : 2 : 2 : 3, a total of 10, matching the ten hydrogens in C₅H₁₀O₂. 1.5 : 1 : 1 : 1.5 has the right proportions but totals only 5, 6 : 4 : 4 : 6 totals 20, and 2 : 3 : 3 : 2 swaps the larger and smaller signals.", "image": "spectra/B1-2.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₅H₁₀O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 3.67, 2.30, 1.66 and 0.95 ppm; integrals under them read 1.48, 1.00, 0.99, 1.51 from left to right."},
  "B1-3": {"id": "B1-3", "set": "B", "spectrum": "B1", "step": 3, "stepName": "Chemical shift", "question": "What does the shift of the 2H signal at 2.30 ppm tell you?", "options": ["The CH₂ is bonded to the ester oxygen", "The CH₂ is part of a C=C double bond", "The CH₂ is in an alkyl chain, away from any O or C=O", "The CH₂ is next to the C=O group"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "A CH₂ next to a carbonyl group is moderately deshielded and typically appears at 2.0 to 2.7 ppm, so 2.30 ppm fits CH₂–C=O. A CH₂ bonded to the ester oxygen would sit near 4.0 ppm, alkene hydrogens appear above 4.5 ppm, and a CH₂ further along the chain, like the one at 1.66 ppm, appears at about 1.3 to 1.7 ppm.", "image": "spectra/B1-3.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₅H₁₀O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 3.67, 2.30, 1.66 and 0.95 ppm; integrals under them read 1.48, 1.00, 0.99, 1.51 from left to right. The signal at 2.30 ppm is marked."},
  "B1-4": {"id": "B1-4", "set": "B", "spectrum": "B1", "step": 4, "stepName": "Coupling", "question": "What does the splitting of this signal show?", "options": ["It has 1 hydrogen on the neighbouring carbon", "It has 2 hydrogens on the neighbouring carbon", "It has 3 hydrogens on the neighbouring carbon", "It is coupled to the carbonyl carbon"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The enlarged signal is a triplet: three lines with intensities about 1 : 2 : 1. By the n + 1 rule, three lines means two equivalent neighbouring hydrogens, so this CH₂ sits next to another CH₂. Counting the lines themselves as neighbours is the commonest slip. The carbonyl carbon is almost always ¹²C, which has no nuclear spin, so it does not split the signal.", "image": "spectra/B1-4.png", "caption": "¹H NMR spectrum of C₅H₁₀O₂, 400 MHz, CDCl₃, with the signal at 2.30 ppm enlarged. Simulated.", "alt": "Simulated ¹H NMR spectrum (400 MHz, CDCl₃) with signals at 3.67, 2.30, 1.66 and 0.95 ppm; integrals under them read 1.48, 1.00, 0.99, 1.51 from left to right. An enlarged view shows the signal at 2.30 ppm as three lines with heights in the ratio 1 : 2 : 1."},
